   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Results achieved</narrative>
    <narrative xml:lang="SV">Uppn&#xe5;dda resultat</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">WFP&apos;s global results in 2025

In 2025, Sida provided support to WFPs crisis response amounting to 314,000,000 SEK, as well as 108,500,000 SEK to UNHAS (United Nations Humanitarian Air Service). The following crises received earmarked support from Sida: Afghanistan, Bangladesh, Burkina Faso, Democratic Republic of Congo, Haiti, Mali, Mozambique, Myanmar, Niger, Nigeria, Sudan, South Sudan, Syria, Venezuela and Yemen. 

General observations
In 2025, WFP assisted more than 120 million people facing severe hunger and malnutrition  equivalent to 101 percent of the revised targets  of whom 79 million received emergency assistance, representing a reduction of 3.5 million people reached compared with 2024. WFP distributed 2.3 million metric tonnes of food in 69 countries, representing 47 percent of the needs based requirements, as well as USD 2.2 billion in cash based transfers (CBTs) and commodity vouchers in 77 countries, equivalent to 47 percent of the planned target. WFP also provided lifesaving malnutrition prevention and treatment to nearly 23 million people in 24 countries, equivalent to 53 percent of those requiring support. In addition, WFP contributed logistical support and common services, including coordination of cargo movements and facilitation of staff movements to hard-to-reach areas.

Due to funding constraints and access challenges, WFP had to make difficult choices, including reducing ration sizes and the duration of assistance, closing health facilities for the Targeted Supplementary Feeding Programme, and suspending blanket nutrition support for children. WFP primarily prioritised people facing Integrated Food Security Phase Classification (IPC) levels 4 and 5  equivalent to 25 percent of the total number of acutely food insecure people in the target countries  which affected both the quantity and quality of assistance, including its nutritional adequacy.</narrative>
